🤖 Agent: Great — congrats again on baby Jasper! I’ll lay out a few practical systems (digital + physical) and quick how-tos so you can pick the workflow that fits you best. If you tell me which calendar/app you use (Google/Apple/Outlook/other), I can give step-by-step instructions or even draft a template for you.

Quick goals for the system
- Capture birthdays & birthdates once (add to Contacts if possible).  
- Set recurring reminders with the lead time you want (cards/gifts/shipping).  
- Track milestones (babies, anniversaries, graduations) and notes/gift ideas.  
- Make it easy to share with family or keep private.

Digital calendar approach (recommended)
1. Create a dedicated calendar  
   - Make a separate calendar called “Birthdays & Milestones” so you can toggle it on/off.  
2. Add each event as a recurring event  
   - Event title: “Ava — Birthday” or “Jasper Born (David)”  
   - Date: birthday or birthdate. Set recurrence = Yearly.  
   - Description: add relation, gift ideas, typical card/message, preferred shipping lead time.  
3. Add reminders/notifications  
   - Examples: 4 weeks (start shopping/ordering), 1 week (wrap/ship), 1 day (send card). Use both popup + email if you want.  
4. Use the contact DOB field (if you save people in your Contacts)  
   - Many calendars can auto-show birthdays from Contacts; that avoids double entry.  
5. Sync & share  
   - Sync across phone/tablet/computer. Optionally share the calendar with family members (e.g., “Family Birthdays”) so everyone sees reminders.  
6. Automation (optional)  
   - Import Facebook birthdays (if you use) or use Zapier/IFTTT to add birthdays from a spreadsheet or a form.

Apple / Google / Outlook quick tips
- Google Calendar: Create calendar (left panel > + > Create new calendar). Add event > More options > Repeat > Custom > Yearly. Add notifications under “Add notification.”  
- Apple Calendar: File > New Calendar or use iCloud > Add event > Edit > repeat yearly > alert(s) as needed.  
- Outlook: Calendar > New Calendar > New Appointment > Recurrence > yearly > set reminders.

Physical + hybrid options
- Use a wall/desk family calendar for quick visual at home (mark big events, birthdays).  
- Keep a small “Birthday & Milestones” notebook or a printed yearly planner with notes on gift history and preferences.

A simple tracking spreadsheet (keeps extra context)
Columns: Name | Relation | DOB | Event Type | Reminders set (4w/1w/1d) | Gift ideas | Last year gift | Notes (e.g., allergies, favorite stores)  
- This is great if you want long-term history (what you gave previous years).

Managing baby & new additions (like Jasper)
- If you don’t know exact dates, add a placeholder and update when you confirm.  
- For babies, add milestones columns: 1st month, 3 months, 6 months, 1 year — and reminders for “first-year card” or first-birthday gift.

Apps and tools (if you want extras)
- Todoist, Any.do or Remember The Milk: set recurring tasks for planning/ordering gifts.  
- Trello or Notion: if you want a richer database with images (gift ideas) and collaborative boards.  
- Paper: Moleskine or a “Book of Dates” journal if you prefer handwriting.

Message/gift prep
- In each calendar event description you can paste a short message template or sample card text so you’re ready to go.  
- Add links to preferred gifts/stores in the event description for quick ordering.

Keep guilt-free boundaries
- If you prefer celebrating in small ways, add that preference to the note (e.g., “Ava — small card only”) so you won’t overcommit.

