What it can help
- Mild–moderate inflammatory acne (reduces bacteria + inflammation)  
- Minor fungal issues (e.g., athlete’s foot) and dandruff (some evidence)  
- Occasional spot‑treatment of pimples

Evidence note
- Clinical studies have shown topical formulations (e.g., ~5% tea tree gel) can reduce acne and are often gentler than benzoyl peroxide, though they work more slowly.

Safe application & dilution
- Never apply undiluted (neat) tea tree oil to the skin — it commonly causes irritation or allergic contact dermatitis.  
- Typical safe dilutions for facial use: 1%–5%. Start low (1%–2%) if you have sensitive skin.  
  - Quick guide: 1 teaspoon ≈ 5 mL. 1% = ~1 drop tea tree oil in 5 mL carrier; 5% = ~5 drops in 5 mL.  
- Good carriers: jojoba oil, grapeseed oil, fractionated coconut oil, or aloe vera gel (non‑comedogenic choices are best for acne‑prone skin).

Step‑by‑step (spot treatment)
1. Do a patch test: apply the diluted mix to an inner forearm/behind ear and wait 24 hours to check for irritation.  
2. Cleanse the affected area gently and pat dry.  
3. Using a cotton swab, apply the diluted tea tree oil to individual pimples (don’t slather over entire face at first).  
4. Leave on (overnight is fine) and wash off in the morning if you feel any stinging. Start once daily; if tolerated, you can try twice daily.  
5. If you prefer a leave‑on daily product, consider buying a commercial 5% tea tree gel (consistent concentration and formulation).

If you want to make a small bottle of diluted solution for multiple spots:
- For a 5 mL travel bottle: add 1–5 drops of tea tree oil to 5 mL carrier (adjust concentration to your tolerance).

Combining with other acne treatments
- Be cautious combining with benzoyl peroxide, topical retinoids, or acids (AHA/BHA). These can increase irritation when used together. If combining, introduce one product at a time and monitor for irritation. Ask your dermatologist for a combined regimen.

Safety & warnings
- Patch test first. Stop use if you get redness, burning, blistering, or worsening rash.  
- Avoid eyes, nostrils, mouth, and other mucous membranes.  
- Do not ingest tea tree oil — it is toxic if swallowed.  
- Not recommended for very young children.  
- If pregnant or breastfeeding, check with your provider before use.  
- If you have persistent or cystic acne, widespread rash, or severe irritation, see a dermatologist rather than relying on essential oils.

When to consult a professional
- If acne is moderate–severe, cystic, or scarring, see a dermatologist (they can prescribe evidence‑based treatments).  
- If you develop allergy/dermatitis from tea tree oil, stop use and consult a clinician.

Bottom line
Tea tree oil can be a useful, gentle option for spot treatment of mild inflammatory acne when properly diluted (start at ~1% and work up if tolerated). Always patch test and prioritize skin safety; for persistent or severe conditions, get professional medical advice.
